# fixedbugs, M-c-callbacks step 5, 2026-09-05 (defect 013).
#
# SYMPTOM: this program built and died with **exit 139 and an empty stderr** —
# a segfault with nothing said, which design.md §1.12 calls a failure of the
# language rather than of its implementation. It is panel 111's llm-ergonomist's
# registered prediction, come true six hours after panel 111 R4 landed the
# callback permission: R4 made the RIGHT spelling legal, `f: (function() -> ())`,
# which refuses `nullptr` at the call site — and left the WRONG one legal beside
# it, where `ptr` accepts any pointer and `nullptr` is one.
#
# WHY IT COULD NOT BE REFUSED AT COMPILE TIME, and this is the whole reason the
# repair is a runtime one. `sqlite3_exec(callback: nullptr)` is the SAME
# declaration and the SAME argument, and it is **correct**: SQLite documents a
# null callback as *do not call back*, and `examples/sqlite/main.hero` ships it.
# Whether NULL is legal is a property of the C function, not of the type — and on
# Windows it is a property of the C LIBRARY, measured: `atexit(NULL)` is exit 0
# there, because the CRT checks. No compiler on either side of the boundary
# carries that fact.
#
# CAUSE, and it is not this program's: nothing looked at the fault. The SIGSEGV
# reached panel 104's handler, failed both stack-overflow witnesses, and was
# passed on to SIG_DFL.
#
# FIX: `runtime/parts/stack.c` gains one witness beside the stack guard's — the
# faulting **PC**. A program that calls through a null function pointer does not
# touch a bad address, it goes to EXECUTE at address zero, and nothing else
# lands there. Measured on this Mac against the three adjacent shapes, one probe:
# a wild store to 0 and a wild read from 0 both give `si_addr == 0` with a
# NONZERO pc, and a garbage function pointer gives a nonzero pc too. Three of the
# four give `si_addr == 0`; exactly one gives `pc == 0`. `si_addr` is the witness
# panel 104 already rejected once, for the same reason.
#
# NOW: `panic: a null function pointer was called …`, exit 134, and the three
# adjacent fault shapes are untouched — a stack overflow still says
# `stack exhausted in deep.down`, an out-of-range index still says
# `array index out of range`.
#
# **There is no `.expected` and no `run/` case on purpose**: this program dies on
# macOS and Linux and exits 0 on Windows, where the CRT checks the pointer, so a
# run golden would be red on one leg by design. The behaviour is asserted by the
# measurements in `docs/work/DONE.md` and by this case existing and emitting.

extern "stdlib.h"
    function atexit(f: ptr) -> i32

function main()
    print("before")
    _ = atexit(nullptr)
